    <section class="content-section animate-in" aria-labelledby="thinking-title">
      <div>
        <h2 id="thinking-title">What I am thinking about</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          AI is becoming cheap and plentiful. The hard part is moving from an
          answer to a useful decision: asking a worthwhile question, using data
          you can actually trust, and building a workflow that survives contact
          with reality.
        </p>
        <p>
          In finance, the model is only one piece of the system. The less
          visible parts — data lineage, point-in-time correctness, validation,
          portfolio construction, execution, risk, and knowing when to do
          nothing — usually decide whether the result is useful.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same applies to most serious software. A good system should not
          merely produce an answer. It should preserve the evidence,
          assumptions, uncertainty, constraints, and consequences behind the
          decision.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section animate-in" aria-labelledby="collaborate-title">
      <div>
        <h2 id="collaborate-title">Work with me</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          I work selectively with funds, family offices, and investment
          technology teams where portfolio judgment, quantitative systems, and
          institutional infrastructure genuinely overlap.
        </p>
